Core reason behind the arrows:
Across a period  → nucleus gains protons (Z ↑) while n stays same → Z_eff ↑ → electrons held tighter
Down a group     → new electron shell added (n ↑) → size ↑, outer e⁻ farther from nucleus

PERIODIC TABLE DIRECTIONAL TRENDS — EQUATIONS & NOTATION
Fundamentals : Z_eff drives almost every arrow
               Across period : Z rises, n fixed  → Z_eff ↑
               Down group    : new shell added   → n ↑

Variables
  Z      atomic number (protons)
  n      principal quantum number (shell)
  σ or S shielding / screening constant
  Z_eff  effective nuclear charge
  r      atomic / orbital radius
  a0     Bohr radius = 0.529 Å
  IE     first ionisation energy
  EA     electron affinity
  χ      electronegativity
  Ed     bond dissociation energy

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
EFFECTIVE NUCLEAR CHARGE
  Z_eff  =  Z  −  σ

  Slater shielding (σ):
    same group (ns/np)     : 0.35 each  (0.30 for 1s)
    n−1 shell              : 0.85 each
    n−2 and inner          : 1.00 each
    nd/nf same group       : 0.35 ; all left of them = 1.00

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ATOMIC RADIUS  (hydrogen-like / Bohr approximation)
  r_n  ≈  n² a0 / Z_eff

  full Bohr form:
  r_n  =  n² ħ² / (Z_eff k_e e² m_e)

  → r decreases L→R (Z_eff ↑, n constant)
  → r increases down group (n ↑)

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
IONISATION ENERGY  (hydrogen-like scaling)
  IE  ≈  13.6  Z_eff² / n²     eV

  orbital energy:
  E_n  =  −13.6  Z_eff² / n²   eV

  → IE generally rises L→R and up a group

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ELECTRONEGATIVITY

  Mulliken (absolute):
    χ_M  =  (IE + EA) / 2

  Pauling (thermochemical difference):
    |χ_A − χ_B|  =  √[ Ed(AB) − (Ed(AA)+Ed(BB))/2 ]

  Allred–Rochow (uses Z_eff directly):
    χ_AR  =  0.359 Z_eff / r²  +  0.744

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
COULOMB FORCE ON VALENCE ELECTRON
  F_eff  =  k  (Z_eff e²) / r²

  larger F_eff  →  smaller r, higher IE, higher χ
